{
  "summary": "The minimal v1 from round 7 is the right direction but has three unresolved gaps that would surface immediately during implementation. First, Kahn's algorithm topological sort is unnecessary given the actual auto-run architecture — the impl_stages loop in server.py (lines 995-1010) processes ONE feature per cycle, breaking after spawning a single executor, so there is no batch to sort; the only thing needed is an is_blocked(feature_id, resolution_map) check added to the existing skip conditions (alongside retry limits, cooldown, already-queued), and the natural iteration order already provides implicit fairness across unblocked candidates. Adding a topological sort over the candidate list adds ~30 lines of graph code that produces the exact same observable behavior as a simple 'skip if blocked' guard, since only one feature is picked per cycle anyway. Second, the dependency_warning flag proposed as a replacement for plan_invalidation_check has undefined operational semantics: if auto-mode still processes warned features, the flag is purely cosmetic and won't prevent wasted compute on features whose plans are invalidated by a rejected dependency; if auto-mode skips warned features, it's functionally identical to blocking but without the clean unblocking semantics (who clears the warning — the human? auto-mode after re-planning? a stage transition hook?). The simplest resolution is: don't add a warning flag at all for v1. Auto-mode already skips features not in impl_stages, so if a dependency is rejected, the dependent feature's plan may produce suboptimal output but the system self-corrects through its existing review cycle (review stage catches plans built on invalid assumptions). Dependency warnings can be a v2 concern once real usage shows whether stale plans from rejected dependencies are actually a problem in practice. Third, the _cmd_edit_depends_on handler needs to address what happens when a feature with active depends_on entries is itself moved to rejected — the dependents of the rejected feature won't know unless they re-resolve on the next auto-run cycle, which is fine for auto-mode but means the TUI/WebUI blocked status display could be stale until the next broadcast. The broadcast triggered by _cmd_move_feature already handles this (it recomputes state), but only if resolve_dependencies is called inside the broadcast state builder rather than cached — so the 'no dirty-flag caching' decision from round 7 is actually load-bearing for correctness, not just a simplicity preference. Make this explicit in the implementation spec."
}
